<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Aristarchus of Samos</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 310–c. 230 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/3rd-century-bce">3rd century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Aristarchus of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samos">Samos</a> was a Greek astronomer and mathematician of the third century BCE. He was the first known thinker to propose that the Earth and planets revolve around the Sun, an idea that found little acceptance in antiquity. His surviving treatise On the Sizes and Distances of the Sun and Moon estimates these by geometry, and Copernicus knew of his heliocentric theory.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/aristarchus-of-samos.jpg" alt="Aristarchus of Samos" width="200" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Claudio Camozzi · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Aristarco.PNG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Aristarchus was born on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samos">Samos</a> in approximately 310 BCE. He likely moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexandria">Alexandria</a> and became a student of Strato of Lampsacus, who later headed the Peripatetic school in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>. Ptolemy reports that Aristarchus observed the summer solstice of 280 BCE, and Vitruvius writes that he built two kinds of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sundial">sundial</a>, one a flat disc and one hemispherical.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>His only surviving work, On the Sizes and Distances of the Sun and Moon, rests on a geocentric view. Aristarchus claimed that at half moon the angle between the Sun and Moon was 87 degrees, and with correct geometry but this inexact figure concluded that the Sun was between 18 and 20 times farther from the Earth than the Moon. The true angle is close to 89 degrees 50 minutes, and the Sun is about 400 times as far. Astronomers up to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tycho-brahe">Tycho Brahe</a> used the solar parallax that his figure implies. From a lunar eclipse he also worked out that the Moon's diameter is roughly one third of the Earth's.</p><p>His heliocentric work is lost and is known from a passage in The Sand Reckoner of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/archimedes">Archimedes</a>. According to it, Aristarchus supposed that the fixed stars and the Sun stay unmoved and that the Earth revolves around the Sun in a circle. Because no stellar parallax could be seen, he held that the stars were other suns, very far away, which implied a much larger universe. He drew on the central fire of Philolaus of Croton and recast it as the Sun.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plutarch">Plutarch</a> says that Aristarchus offered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heliocentrism">heliocentrism</a> only as a hypothesis, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seleucus-i-nicator">Seleucus</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seleucia">Seleucia</a>, a century later, held it as a definite opinion. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plato">Plato</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aristotle">Aristotle</a> and Ptolemy preferred the geocentric model, which was believed through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a>. According to Lucio Russo, the common notion that his contemporaries thought his view sacrilegious comes from a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/printing-press">printing</a> of a passage of Plutarch with words transposed. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicolaus-copernicus">Nicolaus Copernicus</a> knew that Aristarchus had a theory of a moving Earth, though he probably did not know it was heliocentric. A lunar crater, the minor planet 3999 Aristarchus and the telescope Aristarchos bear his name.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Aristarchus of Samos” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Aristarchus of Samos</b>, working in the third century BCE, proposed that the earth moves around the sun, an idea that anticipated Copernicus by some eighteen centuries.</blockquote><a href="/empire/samos">Samos · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samos">Samos</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/greece">Greece</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexandria">Alexandria</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/egypt">Egypt</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=37.75,26.83&amp;year=-258" aria-label="Samos on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Aristarchus of Samos"><image href="/maps/globes/g030n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4786 4336h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4786 4336h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=37.75,26.83&amp;year=-258">What was here in 258 BCE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
